  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 217 PM EDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nThe upper ridge that has been sponsoring our recent hot and mostly \ndry weather is centered off to our west and will be gradually \nbreaking down through the remainder of the weekend into the early \npart of the week, with heights over our area showing a gradual \ndecreasing trend. It remains quite hot this afternoon, with heat \nindex values not far either side of 100 across many southern and \ncentral valley locations. We will see continue to see isolated to\nscattered showers and storms around this afternoon and evening. \nThe atmosphere today features minimal shear, but moderate \ninstability, high PWAT values, steep low level lapse rates and \nDCAPE values either side of 1000 J/kg suggest an environment in \nwhich a few of the stronger storms could produce damaging winds. \n\nModels generally show a bit less coverage of storms Sunday afternoon \nwith an uptick in coverage on Monday, but overall soundings do not \nlook much different from today and we will continue to see the \nthreat of a few storms becoming strong to severe with damaging winds \nthe primary threat. PWAT values will remain high as well, so locally \nheavy rainfall will be possible with a threat of localized flooding. \nIt will also continue to be quite hot for this time of year both \ndays.\n\nAn upper low near the western Great Lakes region will move east and \nsoutheast during the mid week period, and a cold front will move \nthrough our area sometime around Wednesday. We will see an increase \nin showers and storms Tuesday and Wednesday, along with a moderating \ntrend in temperatures as the heat loses its grip on the area.  \n\nWe will be in northwest flow aloft for the latter periods of the \nforecast with upper ridging to our west and a trough centered to our \neast. We may still have a few showers and storms around, but overall \nThursday through Saturday should be drier and cooler. High \ntemperatures are expected to be near to a bit below normal all three \ndays. \n\nLastly, until the expected relief from the heat arrives, Chattanooga \nwill continue to build onto their record streak of 90 degrees or \nhigher. They have climbed above 90 again today so they currently sit \nat 47 days, with a few more days likely to add to the streak. The \nfirst day where the official forecast is below 90 is Wednesday. If \nthey hold on to the 90+ streak through Tuesday, that would bring the \ntotal up to 50 days.",
